hat might be dangerous because someone might be collecting W4. But since I have two W4, discarding one might be risky if someone needs it for pung. However, with 4 left, it's not too tight. But considering opponents' melds: across has W1-W3 and W7-W9 chows, so they might need W4-W6? Possibly, but they already have two W chows, so less likely. Next player has no W melds. Previous has W6-W8 chow, so they might need W4 or W5 for another chow? Possibly. But previous already has W6-W8, so W4-W5 could be used for W4-W5-W6? They might be collecting W. But previous's history shows they played W3, W1, W2, so they might not be collecting W. Hard to tell. Overall, B7 seems safest because B suit is depleted and opponents have B melds already, reducing need for B7. But B7 is a terminal? B7 is 7, not terminal (terminal is 1 or 9). So it's simple. I'll consider discarding B7 first. But let's evaluate each option quickly:  \n- Play B7: discards a non-T tile, keeps T tiles and W tiles. But I want to discard W tiles too eventually.  \n- Play W5: discards a W tile, keeps B7 and W4. But B7 is also non-T.  \n- Play W4: discards a W tile, but I have two, so discarding one reduces potential liability.  \nI think the best is to discard B7 first because it's the only B tile and B suit is unlikely to help me. Then next turn, discard W tiles. But I have two W4 and one W5, so maybe discard W5 first because it's a single, and keep the pair of W4? But I don't need a pair of W4 because I already have a pair of T3. Keeping W4 pair might be useful if I need to change pair later, but for Half Flush, I should not keep W tiles. So discard W4 or W5. Which is safer? W5 has been played twice (by me and across), so it's safer. W4 unseen, might be more dangerous. So discard W4? But I have two, discarding one might signal I have another, but that's okay. Actually, discarding a tile that hasn't been discarded might be risky if someone is waiting for it. But with 4 unseen, it's not too risky. I'll go with discarding B7 as it seems least useful and safe.  \n\nBut let's check if there's any immediate risk: next player's history includes Chi B7? Actually, next player's history: \"Chi B7\" means they claimed a B7 to complete a chow? Wait, \"Chi B7\" in history means they performed a Chi action that completed the sequence including B7? The notation \"Chi B7\" indicates the completed Chow sequence is [B6, B7, B8] or [B5, B6, B7] or [B7, B8, B9]? Typically, \"Chi X\" means the middle tile of the sequence, but the description says it identifies the completed Chow sequence, not the discarded tile taken. So for example, if they did \"Chi B7\", it means they completed a Chow that includes B7, likely [B6,B7,B8] or [B5,B6,B7] or [B7,B8,B9]. Looking at next player's melds: they have [B6,B7,B8] as a chow. So that \"Chi B7\" probably corresponds to that meld. So they already have a B6-B8 chow, so B7 is part of that. They also have a B5 pung. So they have two B melds. They might be going for B suit hand? Possibly, but they already have two B melds, so discarding B7 might be safe because they already used B7. However, they might need another B7 for something else? Unlikely. So B7 is probably safe.  \n\nNow, considering efficiency: I need two more melds. If I discard B7, I keep all T tiles and W tiles. But keeping W tiles might clutter my hand. Actually, I should discard W tiles first because they are more likely to form melds if I draw W tiles, but I'm not aiming for W suit. So discarding W5 or W4 is better for hand purity. But if I discard a W tile, I still have B7 which is useless. So maybe discard B7 first, then W next. But which W? I have two W4 and one W5. The pair of W4 could be a pair if I abandon T3 pair, but I already have T3 pair. So keeping a pair of W4 is redundant and uses two slots. It might be better to discard one W4 to reduce redundancy and keep W5 as a single? Or discard W5 and keep W4 pair? Keeping a pair of W4 gives me flexibility to use either T3 or W4 as pair, but that uses extra tiles. Since I need two melds, having extra pairs isn't helpful unless I go for Seven Pairs, but that's 24 points but difficult. Seven Pairs requires seven pairs, I currently have maybe one pair (T3) and potential pair (W4), but far from seven. So not feasible. So I should aim for standard four melds + pair. So I should keep only one pair. So I should discard one of the W4 to avoid having two pairs? Actually, having two pairs is okay temporarily, but eventually I need to discard one. So discarding one W4 now is fine.  \n\nLet's compare:  \nOption 1: Play B7. Keeps W4,W4,W5,T2,T3,T3,T8. Then I have 7 tiles: two W4, one W5, T2, two T3, T8. Still two W tiles. Next draw, if I draw a T tile, great. If I draw a W tile, I might have to discard it. So I'll still have W tiles.  \nOption 2: Play W5. Keeps W4,W4,B7,T2,T3,T3,T8. Still two W4 and B7.  \nOption 3: Play W4. Keeps W4,W5,B7,T2,T3,T3,T8. One W4, one W5, B7.  \n\nOption 3 reduces W4 to one, but still has W5 and B7. Option 2 keeps two W4. Option 1 keeps two W4 and W5. So Option 3 seems best because it reduces redundancy of W4 and keeps only one W4, one W5, one B7. But I still have three non-T tiles. Option 1 has three non-T tiles (two W4, one W5). Option 2 has three non-T tiles (two W4, one B7). So all have three non-T tiles. But Option 3 has one W4, one W5, one B7 \u2014 more variety, which might be harder to discard later? Actually, having variety means I have to discard each suit separately, which might be safer because I can discard tiles that are safe. But for hand purity, it's worse because I have three different non-T tiles. Ideally, I want to discard all non-T tiles quickly. So perhaps I should discard one non-T tile each turn. So which non-T tile to discard now? I should discard the one that is least useful and safest. B7 seems least useful because B suit is depleted. W5 and W4 have potential to pair with each other? W4 and W5 are consecutive, so if I keep both, they could form part of a chow if